; Silence bootc's expected mac_admin probe in ordinary services (#1198), and
; systemd-nsresourced's dac_read_search denial on its own domain (#1329).
;
; `bootc status` probes for mac_admin by running chcon with a label the policy
; does not define (bootc lsm.rs, selinux_ensure_install). Outside install_t
; the probe is denied, bootc re-executes itself once and probes again, then
; carries on: the denial is the answer it expects. Run from a plain service
; (common's projectbluefin-countme, any script that calls bootc) it logs two
;
;   avc: denied { mac_admin } capability=33
;        scontext=system_u:system_r:unconfined_service_t:s0 tclass=capability2
;
; on every boot. A service whose ExecStart is a shell (/bin/sh <script>) runs
; in initrc_t instead (init_t shell_exec_t:process initrc_t) and logs the same
; pair there: the installer's first-boot fisherman-boot-receipt.service runs
; `bootc status` that way (#1129). dontaudit, not allow: granting mac_admin
; would let every such service write labels the policy does not know, and
; would change what bootc's probe decides. A capability check has no target
; but the process itself, so a domain plus one permission is the narrowest
; rule the policy language can express. Installed at build time (the store
; is on read-only /usr once booted, #551); the desktop contract's [selinux]
; modules list asserts it is loaded.
(dontaudit unconfined_service_t self (capability2 (mac_admin)))
(dontaudit initrc_t self (capability2 (mac_admin)))

; systemd-nsresourced checks dac_read_search on its own process while it
; walks user namespace state at boot, and the policy denies it. The daemon
; carries on without it; the testsuite's SELinux gate counted the denial and
; failed the utah and utah-nvidia flavors of Post-Testing E2E run 44791358885
; (the gaming flavors passed) (#1329). Same shape as the rules above: a
; dontaudit of one capability on the daemon's own domain, nothing granted.
(dontaudit systemd_nsresourced_t self (capability (dac_read_search)))
